## {{senseid|en|forbidden fruit}}{{lb|en|Christianity}} According to [[postbiblical]] [[Christian#Adjective|Christian]] [[tradition#Noun|tradition]], the fruit of the [[tree of knowledge]] which was [[eat#Verb|eaten]] by [[Adam and Eve]] despite [[God]] [[command#Verb|commanding]] them not to do so; the [[forbidden fruit]]. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IX|page=279|lines=485–487|passage=Him by fraud I have ſeduc'd / From his Creator, and the more to increaſe / Your wonder, with an '''Apple'''; {{...}}|year=1873}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1976|author=w:Joni Mitchell|title=Song for Sharon
|passage=Sharon you've got a husband / And a family and a farm / I've got the '''apple''' of temptation / And a diamond snake around my arm}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1985|author=w:Barry Reckord|title=The White Witch|passage=Woman ate the '''apple''', and discovered sex, and lost all shame, and lift up her fig—leaf, and she must suffer the pains of hell. Monthly.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|botany}} {{synonym of|en|pome|t=a [[type#Noun|type]] of fruit in which the often [[edible#Adjective|edible]] [[flesh#Noun|flesh]] [[arise#Verb|arises]] from the [[swollen#Adjective|swollen]] [[base#Noun|base]] of the [[flower#Noun|flower]] and not from the [[carpel]]s}}
