#: {{synonyms|en|malus}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1913|author=John Weathers|title=Commercial Gardening|page=38|passage=If the grafted portion of an '''Apple''' or other tree were examined after one hundred years, the old cut surfaces would still be present, for mature or ripened wood, being dead, never unites.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=P. A. Thomas|title=Trees: Their Natural History|page=227|passage=This allows a weak plant to benefit from the strong roots of another, or a vigorous tree (such as an '''apple''') to be kept small by growing on 'dwarfing rootstock'.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Sid Gardner|title=The Faults of the Owens Valley|isbn=9781440177927|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gcbQzBOnc0YC&pg=PA34|passage=Used to be apple orchards, used to be the river and irrigation ditches that watered the '''apples''', used to be mining towns.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Terri Reid|title=The Everything Guide to Living Off the Grid|page=77|passage=Other fruit trees, like '''apples''', need well-drained soil.}}
# {{synonym of|en|applewood|t=the [[wood#Noun|wood]] of the [[apple tree]]}} {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
